Sure! Below is a quick template you can copy‑paste into your JavaScript environment (or adapt to whatever framework you’re using). Just replace the placeholder strings with your real data/objects.

```js
// Example usage of the ChartSeriesGenerator helper
(async () => {
  try {
    const result = await functions.ChartSeriesGenerator({
      // 1️⃣ Axis labels – this should be a JS array literal as a *string*
      labels: "['January', 'February', 'March', 'April']",

      // 2️⃣ Data points – again a JS array literal as a *string*
      data: "[120, 150, 180, 200]",

      // 3️⃣ Default color – optional, but you can supply a CSS color name or hex value
      color: "'steelblue'",   // <-- note the single quotes inside the outer string

      // 4️⃣ The chart layout object – this is usually a plain JS object describing
      //    things like title, axes, etc. Pass it as a stringified object.
      chartLayout: `
        {
          title: { text: 'Quarterly Sales' },
          xAxis: { categories: ['Q1', 'Q2', 'Q3', 'Q4'] },
          yAxis: { title: { text: 'Revenue ($k)' } }
        }
      `
    });

    console.log('ChartSeries created:', result);

    // If you need to attach the returned series to an existing chart instance:
    // myChartInstance.addSeries(result);   // depends on your chart library

  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Error creating ChartSeries:', err);
  }
})();
```

### What you’ll need to change

| Placeholder | What to put in |
|-------------|----------------|
| `"['January', ...]"` | Your own array of axis labels (e.g., months, categories). Keep it as a **string** containing a valid JS array literal. |
| `"[120, 150, ...]"` | Your numeric data points, also as a **string** array literal. |
| `"'steelblue'"` | Any CSS color string (`'red'`, `'#ff0000'`, `'rgb(255,0,0)'`). |
| `"{ ... }"` | A full chart layout object (title, axes, etc.) formatted as a **string**. |

> **Tip:** If you’re working in a Node.js module or a browser script that already has access to the `functions` namespace, you can drop the `await` and handle the promise differently—just make sure you’re calling the function correctly.

